# Frontend Scenario Rules

## 1. Project Structure

- Next.js App Router: `app/` for routes, `src/components/` for shared UI, `src/lib/` for utilities.
- Vite SPA: `src/pages/` for route components, `src/components/` for UI, `src/stores/` for state.
- Co-locate tests: `button.test.tsx` next to `button.tsx`.
- One component per file. Named exports preferred.

## 2. Image Optimization

- Use framework-native components: `<Image>` (Next.js), `<vite-imagetools>` (Vite).
- AVIF first, WebP fallback, PNG/JPEG last resort.
- Responsive `srcset` with at least 3 breakpoints (mobile, tablet, desktop).
- Always set explicit `width` and `height` to prevent CLS.
- Lazy load all below-the-fold images. `fetchpriority="high"` for hero images only.

## 3. Font Loading

- Self-host fonts via `next/font` or `@fontsource`. No Google Fonts CDN (privacy, perf).
- Preload critical font weights (400, 700 for body; custom weights for headings).
- `font-display: swap` for all fonts. Accept FOUT, avoid FOIT.
- Maximum 2 font families per project unless justified.

## 4. Icon System

- Prefer `lucide-react` or `lucide-vue` — tree-shakeable, consistent design.
- Custom icons: use SVG sprite sheet with `<use href="sprite.svg#icon-name">`.
- SVGR for importing SVGs as React components when custom styling is needed.
- All icons must have `aria-hidden="true"` or `aria-label` for accessibility.

## 5. Routing Type Safety

- Next.js: `pathpida` or built-in typed routes for type-safe navigation.
- Vue: `unplugin-vue-router` for file-based typed routes.
- SvelteKit: built-in typed routes.
- Never use raw string paths for internal navigation.

## 6. Component Organization

- Presentational components receive props and render UI — no side effects.
- Container components own data fetching and state — delegate rendering to presentational.
- Custom hooks for shared logic: `useAuth()`, `useTheme()`, `useMediaQuery()`.
- Compound components for complex widgets (e.g., `<Select.Trigger>`, `<Select.Options>`).
